# invariant Dependency text is inert quoted data

Every dependency-controlled description, policy note, contact field, and surface reference is
labelled with module id plus locked commit and rendered as quoted/data text inside the managed
block. It must never become an imperative sentence addressed to the consuming agent.

# invariant Sanitized text cannot escape the managed block

Before interpolation, the sanitizer removes both HTML-comment delimiter tokens, replaces every
Unicode control rune with a space, collapses whitespace, escapes Markdown table pipes, and
truncates by runes with an explicit `…[truncated]` marker. Limits are 600 runes for module
descriptions and 300 for notes and other fields. Sanitizing is idempotent, and no input can
create `<!-- git-a2a:begin -->` or `<!-- git-a2a:end -->` in generated content.
The exported machine-text sanitizer applies the same delimiter removal, control stripping,
whitespace normalization, and length bounds before dependency data enters JSON or MCP results;
JSON escaping, rather than Markdown pipe escaping, is the final encoding layer there.

# invariant Managed block replacement is singular and atomic

Rendering rejects a file with a missing or duplicate delimiter. It replaces exactly one
complete managed block, preserves all bytes outside it, and writes through the atomic lockfile
writer. Check mode computes drift without writing.

# decision Managed-block presence includes partial delimiters

`HasManagedBlock` reports presence when either begin or end delimiter exists. Callers use this
only to decide whether a mutation must preserve the roster opt-in; the renderer still rejects a
partial or duplicate block rather than treating malformed ownership markers as absent or creating
a second block.

# test Render containment

- Nested-looking HTML comments cannot reveal either managed delimiter.
- Control characters, Markdown pipes, over-limit Unicode text, and hostile dependency fields
  remain inert and bounded.
- Re-rendering the same inputs produces identical bytes.

# decision Managed rosters point to the compact agent briefing

The last content line before the managed end delimiter is exactly “Run `git-a2a usage` to learn
the tool.” It points a newly arrived agent to task instructions without copying a long CLI manual
into every AGENTS.md. Dependency-controlled data always appears before this trusted local line.

# decision Vendored rosters state clone and repair duty

A dependency with lock `vendor` names its repository-relative path and mode in trusted generated
text. Submodules additionally say to clone with `--recurse-submodules` and repair an existing clone
with `git submodule update --init` or `git-a2a wire`; copies need no Git clone instruction. This
state comes from the consumer lock, never from dependency-controlled prose.

# decision Contact budgets are published guidance, not enforcement

When `policy.contact-budget` is present, roster rendering sorts its open-vocabulary keys for
deterministic output and labels the values as a published expectation that git-a2a does not
enforce. A dependency's keys and values pass through the same bounded sanitizer as every other
dependency-controlled field before entering the managed block.

# decision Plain dependencies render identity without invented ownership

A `manifest: none` lock entry renders as a dependency heading marked “plain git dependency”. It
may render trusted consumer-owned vendoring state, but never inferred metadata. When the consumer
explicitly declares a shim, the roster renders its notes and routing table after the exact marker
“described by this repository's shim, not by the dependency”; this content is trusted local input,
not dependency-controlled text. An absent cache for that entry is expected and does not make roster
rendering fail.
